  "state": {
    "case_id": "33",
    "case_summary": "A 30-year-old male presents to the emergency department with a complaint of abdominal pain. The patient states he was at a barbecue around noon. Lunch was arranged as a buffet without refrigeration. Within 2 to 3 hours, he had abdominal pain with 3 episodes of watery diarrhea. On exam, vital signs are T 99.1, HR 103, BP 110/55, RR 14. Abdominal exam is significant for pain to deep palpation without any rebounding or guarding. There is no blood on fecal occult testing (FOBT). What is the most likely cause of this patient's presentation?\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. food poisoning\nB. viral gastroenteritis\nC. bacterial enteritis\nD. irritable bowel syndrome (IBS)\nE. Staphylococcus aureus\nF. None\nG. None\n",
